    <h2>1. Elementary Logic Gates from Universal NAND Primitives</h2>
    <p>Because the two-input <code>Nand</code> gate (<code>out = !(a &amp; b)</code>) is functionally complete, all foundational Boolean logic gates—<code>Not</code>, <code>And</code>, and <code>Or</code>—are constructed exclusively from <code>Nand</code> primitives using Boolean algebra identities and De Morgan’s laws.</p>

    <h3>1.1 NOT Gate (<code>Not.hdl</code>) — Boolean Identity: Not(in) = in&#39; = (in · in)&#39;</h3>
    <p>Tying both inputs of a single <code>Nand</code> gate together applies the idempotent law (<code>in · in = in</code>), producing the logical complement in one gate delay (1 NAND).</p>
    <pre>CHIP Not {
    IN in;
    OUT out;
    PARTS:
    Nand(a=in, b=in, out=out);
}</pre>

    <h3>1.2 AND Gate (<code>And.hdl</code>) — Boolean Identity: And(a, b) = ((a · b)&#39;)&#39;</h3>
    <p>Applying double negation to the output of a <code>Nand</code> gate recovers the logical conjunction using two <code>Nand</code> gates (2 NAND stages).</p>
    <pre>CHIP And {
    IN a, b;
    OUT out;
    PARTS:
    Nand(a=a, b=b, out=w1);
    Nand(a=w1, b=w1, out=out);
}</pre>

    <h3>1.3 OR Gate (<code>Or.hdl</code>) — De Morgan’s Law: Or(a, b) = (a&#39; · b&#39;)&#39; = a + b</h3>
    <p>Two <code>Nand</code> inverters complement inputs <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> in parallel, and a third <code>Nand</code> gate combines them to produce the logical disjunction (2 NAND stages).</p>
    <pre>CHIP Or {
    IN a, b;
    OUT out;
    PARTS:
    Nand(a=a, b=a, out=nota);
    Nand(a=b, b=b, out=notb);
    Nand(a=nota, b=notb, out=out);
}</pre>

