    <h2>4. Hierarchical Sequential Memory (RAM8 &amp; RAM64) &amp; Address Decomposition</h2>
    <p>Sequential memory aggregates clocked 16-bit <code>Register</code> cells into addressable banks. <code>RAM8</code> uses a 3-bit address to demultiplex the write-enable <code>load</code> line across 8 registers and multiplex their outputs. <code>RAM64</code> stacks eight <code>RAM8</code> blocks using a 6-bit address.</p>

    <pre>// RAM8.hdl                                               // RAM64.hdl
CHIP RAM8 {                                               CHIP RAM64 {
    IN in[16], load, address[3];                              IN in[16], load, address[6];
    OUT out[16];                                              OUT out[16];
    PARTS:                                                    PARTS:
    DMux8Way(in=load, sel=address,                            DMux8Way(in=load, sel=address[3..5],
             a=l0,b=l1,c=l2,d=l3,e=l4,f=l5,g=l6,h=l7);                 a=l0,b=l1,c=l2,d=l3,e=l4,f=l5,g=l6,h=l7);
    Register(in=in, load=l0, out=r0);                         RAM8(in=in, load=l0, address=address[0..2], out=ram0);
    Register(in=in, load=l1, out=r1);                         RAM8(in=in, load=l1, address=address[0..2], out=ram1);
    Register(in=in, load=l2, out=r2);                         RAM8(in=in, load=l2, address=address[0..2], out=ram2);
    Register(in=in, load=l3, out=r3);                         RAM8(in=in, load=l3, address=address[0..2], out=ram3);
    Register(in=in, load=l4, out=r4);                         RAM8(in=in, load=l4, address=address[0..2], out=ram4);
    Register(in=in, load=l5, out=r5);                         RAM8(in=in, load=l5, address=address[0..2], out=ram5);
    Register(in=in, load=l6, out=r6);                         RAM8(in=in, load=l6, address=address[0..2], out=ram6);
    Register(in=in, load=l7, out=r7);                         RAM8(in=in, load=l7, address=address[0..2], out=ram7);
    Mux8Way16(a=r0,b=r1,c=r2,d=r3,e=r4,f=r5,g=r6,h=r7,        Mux8Way16(a=ram0,b=ram1,c=ram2,d=ram3,
              sel=address, out=out);                                    e=ram4,f=ram5,g=ram6,h=ram7,
}                                                                       sel=address[3..5], out=out);
                                                          }</pre>

    <h3>4.1 Address Decomposition Workings for RAM64</h3>
    <p>Because 64 = 2<sup>6</sup> = 2<sup>3</sup> × 2<sup>3</sup>, the 6-bit address bus <code>address[0..5]</code> partitions into two orthogonal 3-bit coordinates:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Address Slice</th>
          <th scope="col">Binary Bits</th>
          <th scope="col">Decimal Range</th>
          <th scope="col" class="left">Architectural Function in <code>RAM64</code></th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>address[3..5]</code></td><td><code>a5 a4 a3</code> (MSBs)</td><td>0 – 7</td><td class="left"><strong>Bank Selector:</strong> Drives <code>DMux8Way</code> (to assert <code>l0..l7</code>) and <code>Mux8Way16</code> (to select <code>ram0..ram7</code>).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>address[0..2]</code></td><td><code>a2 a1 a0</code> (LSBs)</td><td>0 – 7</td><td class="left"><strong>Word Offset:</strong> Broadcast to all eight <code>RAM8</code> blocks to select the target <code>Register</code> (0–7) inside the active bank.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p><strong>Worked Example — Global Address 45 (<code>101101<sub>2</sub></code>):</strong> Splitting <code>101101</code> yields upper bits <code>address[3..5] = 101<sub>2</sub> = 5</code> (activating <code>RAM8</code> chip #5 via <code>l5 = load</code> and routing <code>ram5</code> to <code>out</code>) and lower bits <code>address[0..2] = 101<sub>2</sub> = 5</code> (selecting <code>Register</code> #5 inside <code>RAM8</code> #5). Verification: 5 × 8 + 5 = 45.</p>

    <h2>5. Sequential Timing Diagrams &amp; State-Transition Table</h2>
    <p>In Nand2Tetris, clocked chips operate on discrete <strong>tick-tock</strong> cycles: during <code>tick</code> (rising edge), inputs are sampled by internal Data Flip-Flops (<code>DFF</code>); during <code>tock</code> (falling edge / cycle completion), the newly latched state appears at <code>out(t)</code>. Reads are purely combinational (zero-cycle latency after address settles), whereas writes commit at the next clock cycle boundary.</p>

