    <h2>7. Architectural Reflection: Memory Hierarchy &amp; Modern L1/L2 Caches</h2>
    <div class="callout">
      <p>Constructing <code>RAM64</code> from eight <code>RAM8</code> banks illustrates the fundamental engineering trade-off governing real processor memory hierarchies: <strong>capacity versus access latency</strong>. As memory capacity scales up, address decoding trees (<code>DMux8Way</code> / <code>Mux8Way16</code>) deepen logarithmically, wiring capacitance increases, and critical-path propagation delay grows—directly constraining maximum clock frequency.</p>
      <p style="margin-top: 8px;">Modern CPUs resolve this bottleneck using multi-level static RAM caches (<strong>L1 and L2</strong>) positioned physically adjacent to the ALU execution pipeline:</p>
      <ul style="margin-top: 6px;">
        <li><strong>L1 Cache (32–64 KB, 1–4 cycles):</strong> Analogous to a small, shallow register/RAM bank wired directly to the ALU. Its compact footprint minimizes address-decode multiplexers and wire length so operands arrive within a single clock cycle, exploiting <em>temporal and spatial locality</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>L2 Cache (256 KB–2 MB, 10–14 cycles):</strong> Organized hierarchically into multi-bank sets—just like <code>RAM64</code> partitions 64 words across eight <code>RAM8</code> sub-arrays. Splitting the physical address into <em>Tag</em>, <em>Set Index</em> (analogous to <code>address[3..5]</code>), and <em>Block Offset</em> (analogous to <code>address[0..2]</code>) enables parallel bank lookup while isolating idle sub-arrays to conserve dynamic power.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
